1,3,3,6,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,6,3],[3,6,3,3,1,6,3,1,3,3,3,3,3,3,6,3,3,3,6,3,3],[6,3,3,3,6,1,3,3,1,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,6,3,3,3],[3,3,3,6,3,3,1,3,3,1,3,3,3,3,3,3,6,3,3,3,3],[6,3,6,3,3,3,3,1,3,3,1,3,3,3,3,6,3,3,3,3,3],[3,6,3,3,3,3,3,3,1,3,3,3,3,3,6,3,3,3,3,3,3],[6,3,6,3,3,3,3,3,3,1,3,3,3,6,3,3,3,3,3,6,3],[3,3,3,6,3,3,3,3,3,3,1,3,6,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3],[3,3,3,3,6,3,3,3,3,3,3,6,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3],[3,3,6,3,3,6,3,3,3,3,6,3,1,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3],[3,3,3,3,3,3,6,3,3,6,3,3,3,1,3,3,3,3,3,3,3],[3,3,3,3,3,3,3,6,6,1,3,3,3,3,1,3,3,3,3,3,3],[3,3,3,3,3,3,3,6,6,3,3,3,3,3,3,1,3,3,3,3,3],[3,3,3,3,3,3,6,1,3,6,3,3,3,3,3,3,1,3,3,3,3],[3,3,3,3,3,6,1,3,3,3,6,3,6,3,3,3,3,1,3,3,3],[3,3,3,3,6,1,3,3,3,3,3,6,3,3,3,3,3,3,1,3,3],[3,3,3,6,1,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,6,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3],[3,3,6,1,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,1,3,6,3,3,3,3,3,3,3],[3,6,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,6,3,3,3,3,3,3]]}]},"teaching_variant":"canonical","teacher_models":["claude-opus-5-5"],"explanation":"## How the rule works\n\n**The pieces.** Every grid has a single background colour with isolated single cells of colour 1 and colour 6 scattered on it. Nothing else is present.\n\n**Evidence from the demonstrations.**\n\n- **Pair 5 (only two 1s).** The 1s at (2,1) and (7,6) lie on the same main diagonal, since y−x = 1 for both. The output fills the diagonal cells between them with 1. So two 1s that share a diagonal get joined by a diagonal segment of 1s.\n- **Pair 3.** The 1s at (1,7) and (7,1) share an anti-diagonal (x+y = 8), and they are joined the same way. The 6 at (6,7) is not on that segment, and it does nothing.\n- **Pair 2.** The 1s at (1,6) and (6,1) are joined along x+y = 7. The 6 at (3,4) lies on that segment. In the output a full line of 6 runs through it, perpendicular to the segment (the main diagonal y−x = −1), from one grid border to the other.\n- **Pair 1.** The 1s at (3,1) and (9,7) are joined along y−x = 2. That segment passes through the 6s at (5,3) and (8,6). Each of those 6s gets a full perpendicular anti-diagonal of 6: x+y = 8 and x+y = 14. The other 6s, at (2,9) and (13,4), are not on any segment and stay unchanged.\n- **Pair 4.** The 1s at (2,2) and (11,11) are joined, and the 6 at (9,9) on that segment shoots a full line along x+y = 18. The 1s at (3,12) and (14,3) are not on a common diagonal, so they are left alone. The other 6s do nothing.\n\n**The rule.**\n\n1. For every pair of 1-cells on a common diagonal (same y−x or same x+y), fill the background cells between them with 1.\n2. For every 6 lying strictly between such a pair, draw a full diagonal line of 6 through it, perpendicular to the segment, to both borders.\n3. Both kinds of drawing only paint background cells. The pre-existing cells keep their colour; in pair 4, the 6 line ends next to the 1 at (14,3) without touching it.\n\n**On the test input,** the program checks every pair of 1s. Any pair sharing a diagonal gets its segment, and any 6 sitting on that segment emits its perpendicular line. The program does not depend on grid size or absolute positions, and it handles any number of pairs.\n\n**Checkpoints emitted.** One canvas after each 1-segment is drawn, one after each perpendicular 6-line, and the final canvas.","code":"def transform(grid):\n    g = np.array(grid)\n    bg = background(g)\n    H, W = g.shape\n    out = g.copy()\n    ones = [(int(y), int(x)) for y, x in zip(*np.where(g == 1))]\n    sixes = set((int(y), int(x)) for y, x in zip(*np.where(g == 6)))\n    for i in range(len(ones)):\n        for j in range(i + 1, len(ones)):\n            (y0, x0), (y1, x1) = ones[i], ones[j]\n            if abs(y1 - y0) != abs(x1 - x0) or y0 == y1:\n                continue\n            dy = 1 if y1 > y0 else -1\n            dx = 1 if x1 > x0 else -1\n            n = abs(y1 - y0)\n            hit6 = []\n            for k in range(1, n):\n                y, x = y0 + k * dy, x0 + k * dx\n                if (y, x) in sixes:\n                    hit6.append((y, x))\n                elif out[y, x] == bg:\n                    out[y, x] = 1\n            emit(out, f\"Joined the 1s at {(y0, x0)} and {(y1, x1)} with a diagonal of 1s.\")\n            # perpendicular direction\n            pdy, pdx = dy, -dx\n            for (sy, sx) in hit6:\n                for sgn in (1, -1):\n                    y, x = sy + sgn * pdy, sx + sgn * pdx\n                    while 0 <= y < H and 0 <= x < W:\n                        if out[y, x] == bg:\n                            out[y, x] = 6\n                        y += sgn * pdy\n                        x += sgn * pdx\n                emit(out, f\"The 6 at {(sy, sx)} lies on that segment, so a perpendicular diagonal of 6 is drawn through it to the borders.\")\n    emit(out, \"Final grid.\")\n    return out\n","candidate_tactics":{"rays":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"- **Pair 4.** The 1s at (2,2) and (11,11) are joined, and the 6 at (9,9) on that segment shoots a full line along x+y = 18."}],"count":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"The program does not depend on grid size or absolute positions, and it handles any number of pairs."}]},"provenance":{"reference_assisted":false,"query_labels_used_for_rule_diagnosis":false},"artifact_sha256":"c2c05e7b7e3c9fc316edc87032c4df42521cbd1da529236ea7b12c7a30aa54bf","task_sha256":"c785bd3ba6d976f633da07879dda1eb53a54cfe71b2974cb3fdb5bda1cc4611b","validation":"Frozen corpus recorded successful replay on teaching fixtures; synthetic generalization not established."}
